headlines.series:V:B:
\begin{maintheorem}[The integral rank-six construction]
\hypertarget{tag-03KS}{}\label{series:V:B}
\StatementRecord{series:V:B}{proved}{03KS}{\cref{maps:src:F.2.5}, \cref{maps:src:F.2.6}, \cref{night:P1.13}}
The rank-six integral monodromy of \cref{pub:V:rank-six-data}, with free affine fillings of orders five and four and the index-one Kuhn cusp, gives a closed smooth eight-manifold that is three-connected, has free integral homology, and has middle intersection form $3\langle1\rangle\oplus\langle-1\rangle$.

\end{maintheorem}

outline_blocks.B:
\hypertarget{outline-V-B}{}
\subsubsection*{Theorem~\ref{series:V:B}: The integral rank-six construction}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item\hypertarget{step-V-B-1}{} The matrices of \cref{pub:V:rank-six-data} have orders five and four. The square-zero cusp has primitive rank-three image, and its explicit unimodular marking is proved in \cref{night:P1.1}. The invariant covector $x\mapsto x_0$ detects every nonidentity affine power, proving the freeness used in \cref{pub:V:smooth-gluing}.

\item\hypertarget{step-V-B-2}{} The cusp and finite quotient charts give the marked smooth gluing. Its van Kampen relations reduce to $5a=h$, $4b=-h$ and $a+b=0$; their integral relation matrix is unimodular. Hence the manifold is simply connected, by \cref{pub:V:smooth-gluing}.

\item\hypertarget{step-V-B-3}{} The signed-orbit bases at the finite cores, their norm maps, and the primitive cusp collapse give the integral restriction sheaves and their point kernels. Determinant minors compute their cohomology in \cref{pub:V:integral-restrictions}, using the collapse of \cref{night:P1.11}.

\item\hypertarget{step-V-B-4}{} For the order-four core, the loops $v=g^4h^{-1}$ and $t=g$ form an integral basis. A torsion representative pulls back to $(0,w)$, so its transgression is $\pm[w]$ by \cref{mapsaudit:order-four-torsion-transgression}. The three nonzero global images are proved in \cref{pub:V:integral-restrictions}.

\item\hypertarget{step-V-B-5}{} The three torsion differentials are isomorphisms onto $\mathbb Z/2$. The remaining cusp kernel is free of rank one. The Leray filtration, universal coefficients, duality and Hurewicz then give free integral homology and three-connectivity in \cref{maps:src:F.2.6}.

\item\hypertarget{step-V-B-6}{} The almost complex structure has $c_1=c_3=0$. The index and signature identities of \cref{sec:V-known-inputs} force $c_2^2=2$, $\sigma=2$ and the middle form $I_{3,1}$. The Pontryagin identities and characteristic vector are computed in \cref{night:P1.13}.

\end{enumerate}
